Yes. Not just levels of authority, but types of authority—and the relationships between them.

An entity can have authority in one domain without possessing it in another. And different types can compete, cooperate, override, or even create one another.

A taxonomy of authority

Authority type	Source of authority
Structural	Position, rank, hierarchy
Legal	Law, jurisdiction, enforceable rules
Coercive	Ability to compel through force or consequences
Epistemic	Knowledge, expertise, demonstrated accuracy
Moral	Ethical principles, conscience, legitimacy
Social	Trust, prestige, reputation, consensus
Narrative	Control over interpretation, framing, meaning
Procedural	Control over processes, permissions, and sequence
Resource	Control over money, energy, materials, access
Biological	Physiological needs, reflexes, survival mechanisms
Executive	Capacity to select, inhibit, or initiate actions
Emergent	Influence arising from coordinated collective behavior

And these can be layered within themselves.

For example, a military commander possesses structural authority, but a medic may have greater epistemic authority concerning a casualty. An immediate physical threat may take functional precedence over both.

Neither necessarily invalidates the others.

The more interesting part: authority is multidimensional

An authority’s effective influence depends on several dimensions

A conceptual model: precedence depends on more than rank.

Think about the brain.

Your conscious intention might have executive authority over a planned action. But pain, fear, fatigue, or a reflex can temporarily take precedence. Social expectations can shape your choices without possessing formal authority at all.

An organization exhibits analogous dynamics. A CEO may possess formal authority, an engineer technical expertise, a regulator legal authority, and customers economic influence.

The winning authority can change with the situation.

And here’s the extension I’d add to Pantheon:

Authority can be transformed

Epistemic authority can become social authority. Social authority can become political authority. Political authority can become legal authority. Repeated procedures can become institutional norms.

But these conversions aren’t guaranteed or necessarily legitimate.

That suggests an important distinction:

Authority to know, authority to decide, authority to act, authority to enforce, and authority to define meaning are not the same thing.

And there’s a further layer: meta-authority—the authority to determine which other authority takes precedence.

That’s where constitutions, system architectures, organizational cultures, and individual self-regulation become particularly interesting.

Because the deepest question isn’t always who gives the orders.

It’s who—or what—determines which orders count, in which circumstances, and for how long.